olve the inequality $\\ln (x + 2) - 5 \\ge \\ln (3x)$."] [17592186157070 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{3}{5} \\ln(x+2) +1 \\leq 0$."] [17592186157070 :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5}\\ln (x + 2) + 1 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\ln (x + 2) & \\le - 1 \\\\ \\ln (x + 2) & \\le - \\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e$. We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}e^{\\ln (x + 2)} & \\le e^{-5/3} \\\\ x + 2 & \\le e^{-5/3} \\\\ x & \\le e^{-5/3} - 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+2> 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x > -2\n$$\n\nOur solution is\n\n$$\nx \\leq e^{-5/3}-2\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $[MATH: \\left(-\\infty, e^{-5/3}-2\\right].]$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x > -2$, which can be expressed as $(-2, \\infty)$. To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$[MATH: \\left(-\\infty, e^{-5/3}-2\\right] \\cap (-2, \\infty) = \\left(-2,e^{-5/3}-2 \\right]]$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $[MATH: x \\in \\left(-2, e^{-5/3}-2\\right],]$ which can also be written as\n\n$$\n-2< x \\leq e^{-5/3}-2\n$$"] [17592186157071 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\ln(x+2) - 6 < \\ln(x-2)$."] [17592186157071 :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e$. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}e^{\\ln (x + 2) - 6} & < e^{\\ln (x - 2)} \\\\ e^{\\ln (x + 2)} \\cdot e^{-6} & < e^{\\ln (x - 2)} \\\\ (x + 2)e^{-6} & < x - 2 \\\\ (x + 2) & < (x - 2)e^{6} \\\\ (x + 2) & < xe^{6} - 2e^{6} \\\\ 2 + 2e^{6} & < (e^{6} - 1)x \\\\ \\frac{2 + 2e^{6}}{e^{6} - 1} & < x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 2 & > 0 & ⇒ & x & > - 2 \\\\ x - 2 & > 0 & ⇒ & x & > 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nOur solution is\n\n$$\nx > \\dfrac{2+2e^6}{e^6-1}\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2+2e^6}{e^6-1},\\infty \\right)\n$$\n\nAdditionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x > -2$ and $x>2$, which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty)$ and $(2,\\infty)$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2+2e^6}{e^6-1},\\infty \\right) \\cap (-2, \\infty) \\cap (2, \\infty) = \\left(\\dfrac{2+2e^6}{e^6-1}, \\infty \\right)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx \\in \\left(\\dfrac{2+2e^6}{e^6-1}, \\infty \\right)\n$$\n\nwhich can also be written as\n\n$$\nx > \\dfrac{2+2e^6}{e^6-1}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186156323 17592186156324 17592186156325 17592186156326 17592186156327] "immutable_fields" [[17592186156322 :answer-field/correct] [17592186156322 :answer-field/id] [17592186156322 :answer-field/key] [17592186156322 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
